[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It was not one issue. Its
reports describe three different problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October:

- **`parser`**: fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`).
- **`hint`**: won't fix. The booking-v3 form is retired, so its hint goes with it.
- **`one-day`**: not addressed. Neither change touches it.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not every report in it is. The one-day moves are not a date format: no
parser fix and no form change reaches them. Their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get day and
  month swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. At day-first tenants people type the
  month first, as told, and the form reads the day first. Only form bookings at booking-v3
  tenants. The parser fix does not reach it: the form already reads the tenant's way.
- **`one-day`**: the booking lands one day off, not swapped. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
  Client in Toronto got Monday." That is a time-zone shift, not a format. The desk flagged these
  in its notes, but the #4471 page still counts them under the parser.

## How the reports were placed

By the tenant, not by intake's tag. Intake guesses the cause from the reporter's browser; the
cause depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`). The two disagree in both
directions on this ticket. The ticket export also repeats some reports; each id counts once.

## Why one ticket hid three

Triage merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Every one of these reports has a date-sounding subject ("Wrong day on the invite", "Dates mixed
up"), so they all merged into the first open date ticket, whatever the body said. The body is
where the problems differ.

